The '''Jarin national football team''' represents [[Jarin]] in men's international association football. It is governed by the [[Jarin Football Federation]] and is one of the stronger Mesogean sides in the [[AFA Men's World Ranking]], sitting sixth in the June 2026 table. Jarin's football culture is closely associated with the country's Beja-speaking coastal cities, especially [[Shimora]], [[Tokar]], and [[Nakfa]].

Jarin hosted the [[1974 AFA World Cup]] and entered the [[2026 AFA World Cup]] as one of the more experienced teams in the field.

== History ==

=== Early development ===
Jarin's footballing tradition developed around the major urban centres of the southern Mesogean coast. Clubs in [[Shimora]], [[Morentu]], [[Tokar]], and [[Nakfa]] formed the backbone of the domestic game, and the national side became known for a disciplined, organised style that reflected the country's coastal trading culture and strong civic rivalries.

=== Modern era ===
In the modern era, Jarin have remained a consistent Mesogean force and a regular presence near the top of the continental ranking table. The country's hosting of the [[1974 AFA World Cup]] helped establish it as a major football venue, while the 2026 campaign reaffirmed its place among the region's leading teams.

== Stadium ==

Jarin play their home matches at [[Shimora National Stadium]] in [[Shimora]], the country's principal football venue. Important matches are also occasionally staged in [[Tokar]], [[Nakfa]], and [[Morentu]].

== Playing style ==

Jarin are generally described as a compact, tactically disciplined side that relies on organised defending, quick circulation through midfield, and efficient transition play. Their strongest sides have typically combined physical endurance with good structure and strong set-piece execution.

